xnu 0206: the CP table region is large enough for the client buffers

The draw's dump is `fsr 0x80000402 far 0x14a80000` with `pte present 0xe43
pa 0xde81c000`: the software walk finds the page and the SMMU does not. Three
CPU-side TLB probes (ASID invalidate per submission, global `TLBIALLNSNH` in
the map path, `CB_ATS1PR`) were tried first and all are unusable: their SMMU
register reads never returned after the GPU had been idle.

The tables themselves were the problem. They live in a 32-page (128 KiB)
region and the bring-up consumes all 32 (`cp smmu pages 32`), so the first
buffer a client maps that needs a new L2/L3 table allocates it past the end
of the region. `mondrian_gpu_uc_tables_sync()` flushes only
`mondrian_cp_tables.pa .. + bytes`; the new table stays in the CPU cache, the
software walk (which runs on the CPU) reports the PTE present, and the SMMU,
reading DRAM, faults. That is why the 71-dword stream, whose buffers land in
tables the bring-up already wrote, completes, while the 168-dword draw (the
20 MB surface needs a new table) does not.

Grow the region to 512 KiB and refuse a map that would need a table beyond
it, instead of writing past the end.
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
@@ -1742,7 +1742,15 @@
 	if (mondrian_cp_tables.va == 0) {
 		printf("MONDRIAN GPU: cp alloc begin\n");
 		mondrian_log_flush_now();
-		if (mondrian_gpu_mem_alloc(&mondrian_cp_tables, 0x20000, 0) != 0 ||
+		/*
+		 * 512 KiB of tables. The old 128 KiB was exactly what the
+		 * bring-up itself used (`cp smmu pages 32`), so the first buffer
+		 * a client mapped allocated its L2/L3 past the end of the region:
+		 * tables_sync() flushes only the region, the CPU saw the PTE in
+		 * its cache and the SMMU read stale DRAM, which is the draw's
+		 * `fsr 0x80000402` with `pte present`.
+		 */
+		if (mondrian_gpu_mem_alloc(&mondrian_cp_tables, 0x80000, 0) != 0 ||
 		    /* 1 MiB, not the firmware's 0x13000: the CP's icache
 		     * prefetch (and any jump the ucode makes) runs past the
 		     * last byte of the ucode, and a faulting fetch storm
@@ -3037,6 +3045,16 @@
 	    (pa & 0xfffull) != 0) {
 		return -1;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * A map can allocate an L2 and an L3 table. Refuse it when the
+	 * region cannot hold them: writing past the end produced a table
+	 * the flush below never reaches, and the SMMU then faults on a
+	 * page the software walk reports present.
+	 */
+	if (mondrian_uc_table_next + 4 >=
+	    (uint32_t)(mondrian_cp_tables.bytes / 4096)) {
+		return -1;
+	}
 	mondrian_gpu_smmu_map((uint64_t *)mondrian_cp_tables.va,
 	    mondrian_cp_tables.pa, 32, iova, pa, 4096, permissions,
 	    &mondrian_uc_table_next);
